    "result_text": "Tests are green. \n\n## What I changed\n\nI agree with the finding \u2014 the original `for`/`break` loop over `Map.of(...).entrySet()` relied on an iteration order that `Map.of` explicitly does not guarantee, while the Javadoc promised \"longer codes tested before shorter ones.\" That was a real contradiction between the comment and the structure.\n\nRather than force an ordered collection just to make the `break` correct, I made the selection **order-independent**, which is the more robust expression of the intent:\n\n- Added a `longestMatchingCountryCode(digits)` helper that scans the codes and returns the **longest** one the digits start with. This is correct regardless of map iteration order, and it correctly handles the case a future maintainer would worry about \u2014 one code being a prefix of another (e.g. `\"1\"` vs a hypothetical `\"12\"`).\n- `toE164` now selects that code once and validates the national-number length against it.\n- Updated the `NATIONAL_LENGTH` Javadoc to state that order is irrelevant because the longest match is chosen, so comment and code now agree.\n\nAcceptance behaviour is unchanged: `+61 123` (3 national digits) is still rejected with 400, valid `+61`/`+1` numbers still pass, and unknown country codes still fall back to the generic 8\u201315 digit rule. Full suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
