        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe logic lives in the right unit \u2014 `E164Telephone` already owns both normalization and length validation, so extending it with the national-number rule is coherent and additive rather than introducing a new home for phone logic. The `Map` lookup is a natural way to express \"country code \u2192 expected national length.\" One structural problem, though:\n\n**The comment contradicts the data structure it documents.** The Javadoc on `NATIONAL_LENGTH` states:\n\n> Longer codes must be tested before shorter ones so that, for example, `\"61\"` is matched ahead of `\"1\"`.\n\nBut `Map.of(...)` returns an immutable map with **no specified iteration order**. The `for (Map.Entry ... : NATIONAL_LENGTH.entrySet())` loop combined with `break` therefore does *not* honor \"longest code first\" \u2014 the order the entries are visited is undefined and can change between JVM versions. A maintainer reading this will believe an ordering guarantee exists that the code does not provide, and will build on that false assumption. This is a genuine structure/clarity defect regardless of whether the current inputs happen to work.\n\nPick a structure that actually expresses the intent, e.g.:\n- If prefix precedence genuinely matters, iterate an explicitly ordered collection (e.g. a `List` of entries sorted by descending key length, or a `LinkedHashMap`), and let the comment describe that real ordering; or\n- If precedence does *not* matter (each candidate prefix is checked independently), drop the \"must be tested before shorter ones\" rationale entirely so the comment stops promising an order that isn't enforced.\n\nEither way, the comment and the chosen structure should agree. As written they don't, which is exactly the kind of thing that trips up a future maintainer."
